Z||000000||Sndfr||12-03-2000||03:12 PM||||LoL...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Anyways, I have an 84 SE with a pretty well taken care of Iron Duke (2.5 liter, average 8000 miles a year).  Would it be benefitial to use the middle grade fuel instead of the lowest (i think the number is 87)?<P>different question...<BR>Not considering my car, I'm just curious, but what effect does the different grades of fuel have on cars?  Meaning, <I>why</I> would someone use higher grades for a daily driver?<P>------------------<BR>-<B>matthew "Whyt Nyte" d.</B><BR>84 SE<BR><I>"You are not special.<BR>You are not a beatiful and unique snowflake.<BR>You are the same decaying organic matter as everyone else."</I>||||reg||12
Z||000001||theogre||12-03-2000||03:34 PM||||This is very simple in most cases. use the lowest grade fuel that wont cause knock or ping. OR use whatever the manuals say if you have them, especially on newer cars. The car makers all tell you what fuel to use.<P>Older motors with some carbon in them may need a higher octane fuel than original to prevent knock/ping. If you are getting good results from 87 the keep using it. there is no performance benifit to using higher octanes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||12-03-2000||03:42 PM||||To answer your first question, assuming the engine is in good shape and doesn't have significant carbon build up in the cylinders, there is no benefit to using gas with a higher octane number.  Unleaded regular is just fine.<BR>As to your second question.  The main difference between the different grades of gasoline is the octane number.  This is a measure of gasoline's resistance to pre-detonation or "spark knock".  If an engine has been designed to run on 87 octane gas then using a higher octane will not help it at all.  It also won't hurt either.  Some engines are designed for higher octane gas usually due to the compression ratio in the cylinders and some other factors.  Most of them also incorporate some kind of knock sensor that will retard the ignition timing in the event spark knock is detected. Primarily, that is to protect the engine in case the incorrect (lower) grade of gas is used.  Some "premium" grades may have some additives and detergents in them that lower grades may not have depending on the supplier, and these may help the engine to stay cleaner internally, but all else being equall, there is no benefit to using premium in a car designed for regular.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||12-03-2000||03:42 PM||||  I'm not sure about that. I'm not calling you a liar Ogre, but I have had apparently different results. I took the advice of the forum and ran the lower grade gasoline and found that I lost power although my milage went up about 5%. I didn't like the way my car ran so I went back to premium and felt alot better about the performance. Now I'm not sure if this is just an anomaly with my car that has about 109,000 miles and was not taken care of before I bought it. I prefer the higher octane fuel for the performance. My opinion.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||12-03-2000||04:07 PM||||PLOP PLOP FIZZ FIZZ oh what a relief it is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-03-2000||09:03 PM||||For me, this is one that is both right and wrong. I tend to Theogre's position though. Unless the engine has a knock sensor to pull spark, the higher octane gas doesn't do much (and can hurt power in some respects)unless you take advantage of it with more advance.<P>Higher octane gas *allows* more advance, but all things being equal, provides little benefit. A car that is dentonating mildly isn't loosing any power.  Detonation tends to break stuff. I go with the lowest that will not detonate. On the SC's and turbo's you have little choice.||||reg||1
